# The render rules — which SPARQL result cells become controls, and what they do.
#
# ★ This is a RESOURCE, not a constant and not a config key. It is served at
# `urn:iki:gonk:render-rules` (HTTP `/render-rules`, text/turtle), so it can be resolved,
# read, diffed and replaced per deployment: drop your own file at
# `<config home>/gonk/render-rules.ttl` and gonk serves THAT instead of this. A mapping is
# data about how to read this deployment's data; it belongs in the graph, not in the binary.
#
# ★★ A RULE NEVER MATCHES ON A COLUMN NAME ALONE. A SPARQL result set carries whatever
# variable names its author chose, so a rule keyed only on the name works for today's sample
# queries and fails for the query someone writes tomorrow. The rules below match on the
# VALUE wherever the value is unambiguous, and reach for the variable name only where it is
# not — see `rule:item-number`, which says so in its own comment.
#
# Match conditions (a rule applies when EVERY condition it states holds; several values of
# ONE condition are alternatives):
#
#   render:matchKind    "uri" | "literal" | "bnode"   — the term kind the store reported
#   render:matchShape   "ledgerItemIri" | "integer"   — a shape of the value itself
#   render:matchPrefix  "repo:"                       — the value starts with this text
#   render:matchVar     "number"                      — the SPARQL variable this cell is under
#
# Actions (exactly one per rule):
#
#   render:link   "<url template>"      the cell becomes an anchor to that URL
#   render:query  "<SPARQL template>"   the cell becomes an anchor that RUNS that query
#
# Substitutions, in either template and in render:label:
#
#   {value}       the cell's lexical value, as the store returned it
#   {ledger}      the ledger this query ran against
#   {itemLedger}  the ledger named inside a `urn:iki:ledger:…:item:…` value
#   {itemId}      the item id inside such a value
#
# A template whose substitution comes out EMPTY produces no action at all — so a rule that
# reaches for {itemId} without also matching `ledgerItemIri` simply does nothing, rather
# than emitting a broken link.
#
# ⚠ Substitution is ESCAPED FOR THE GRAMMAR IT LANDS IN, always, by the renderer and never
# by the rule: percent-encoding for a `render:link` URL, the SPARQL string-literal grammar
# for a `render:query`. These values come out of the store, and the store holds text other
# people wrote. Writing your own rules does not let you concatenate.
#
# `render:order` is the tie-break; the lowest-numbered matching rule wins, and ties break on
# the rule's IRI so the outcome never depends on parse order.

@prefix rdfs:   <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix render: <urn:iki:gonk:render#> .

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 1. an item IRI links

# The free case: `urn:iki:ledger:default:item:01m2kw5328wttezn` can only be one thing, so no
# heuristic and no false positive is possible. It links whatever the column is called, which
# is the whole of "any query" for item IRIs.
<urn:iki:gonk:render:rule:item-iri>
    a                  render:Rule ;
    rdfs:label         "A ledger item IRI links to that item" ;
    render:order       10 ;
    render:matchKind   "uri" ;
    render:matchShape  "ledgerItemIri" ;
    render:link        "/l/{itemLedger}/item/{itemId}" ;
    render:label       "Open {value}" .

# ------------------------------------------------------------ 2. an item NUMBER links

# ⚠ THE AMBIGUOUS ONE, and it is ambiguous by construction. A SPARQL result carries no
# provenance: in `SELECT ?number ?title (COUNT(?comment) AS ?comments)` the renderer cannot
# tell that 244 came from `ledger:number` and 3 from a COUNT — both are bare integers. A
# value-only rule would turn "3 comments" into a link to item #3.
#
# So this rule, alone among these, consults the variable NAME, and the names are listed here
# rather than compiled in precisely so a deployment whose queries spell it differently can
# add its own without patching gonk. Add yours; do not widen this to every integer column.
<urn:iki:gonk:render:rule:item-number>
    a                  render:Rule ;
    rdfs:label         "A bare integer under a column that names items links to that item" ;
    render:order       20 ;
    render:matchKind   "literal" ;
    render:matchShape  "integer" ;
    render:matchVar    "number", "item", "n", "itemNumber", "item_number" ;
    render:link        "/l/{ledger}/item/{value}" ;
    render:label       "Open item {value} in {ledger}" .

# ------------------------------------------------ 3. a repo label asks the next question

# `repo:ikigai-gonk` is a value shape, so this one needs no column name either. The action
# LEAVES the current result set and asks the ledger a fresh question — the OPEN items with
# that label — rather than filtering the rows already on screen, so an item that was not in
# the original answer still appears. That is the point: a result is somewhere to go next.
<urn:iki:gonk:render:rule:repo-label>
    a                  render:Rule ;
    rdfs:label         "A repo: label runs the open items for that repo" ;
    render:order       30 ;
    render:matchKind   "literal" ;
    render:matchPrefix "repo:" ;
    render:label       "Open items labelled {value}" ;
    # ⚠ ONE interpolation point on purpose: the label lands inside the string literal and
    # nowhere else. A comment is a weaker container than a literal — do not write a
    # placeholder into a comment, here or in a rule of your own. (Keep the query's own
    # comments short for a second reason: the whole text travels in the editor's URL.)
    render:query       """PREFIX ledger: <https://ikigai-rs.dev/ns/ledger#>
PREFIX dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/>

# The open items with this label. Edit the label, or the status, and press Run.
SELECT ?number ?priority ?title WHERE {
  ?item a ledger:Item ;
        ledger:status ledger:open ;
        ledger:label "{value}" ;
        ledger:number ?number ;
        dcterms:title ?title .
  OPTIONAL { ?item ledger:priority ?priority }
}
ORDER BY COALESCE(?priority, 9) ?number""" .
